#---
# purpose: "A dispatch arm is never seated ahead of a sibling holding elements its own surface does not name"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.129 / fz-kdt.131. All three calls reach one
# `List.reduce_while_step/3` callsite, which settles two arms:
#
#     arm A   list(:ok)  x  {Enum.all?/1 lambda, Enum.empty?/1 lambda}
#     arm B   list(int)  x  {Enum.all?/1 lambda}
#
# Arm B's runtime test is strictly INSIDE arm A's -- same list-shape question,
# same `:true` question, and a callable set of one against a set of two. Seat
# the smaller test first and `Enum.all?([:ok, :ok])` satisfies every question
# arm B asks, because a list-shape test cannot see elements, and lands in the
# body compiled for `list(int)`: `fz_list_head_int_ref` reads an atom as an int
# and aborts on `run` and `build`. `interp` prints the right answers anyway --
# its values carry their own tags -- so only the two compiled doors show it.
#
# Neither arm's surface covers the other's at the list position, so no seat is
# escape-free here and the seating rule declines to move the pair: it keeps
# arrival order, which is what these three lines pin. The standing cure is a
# runtime test that can see a list's elements (fz-kdt.107) rather than an
# ordering rule; fz-kdt.131 owns the population.
fn main() do
  dbg(Enum.all?([:ok, :ok]))
  dbg(Enum.empty?([:ok, :ok]))
  dbg(Enum.all?([1, 2]))
end
